course: dictionary
lesson: 12163
title: A formal word: comport
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Comport is a formal word, so you will meet it in serious writing and old stories more than in chatty talk. It has two meanings. The first is about behaviour: to comport yourself means to behave in a particular way. «The guests comported themselves politely at the royal dinner» means they were polite and well-mannered.
explain: The second meaning is about fit. To comport with something means to agree with it or suit it. «Her story comports with the photos we found» means her story matches the photos. The word is a clue: look for the sentence to see which meaning fits.
explain: Use the context check. Put your meaning in place of the hard word and read it again. «The guests behaved politely at the royal dinner» still makes sense, so behave is a good meaning. If the sentence is about a story or a rule, try agree with or match instead.
fact: Comport is a verb, so its forms are comports, comported and comporting.
fact: «Comport yourself» means behave in a particular way, often in a formal place.
fact: «Comport with» means agree with or suit something, as in «His story comports with the facts».
fact: «Comported» tells you that the behaviour happened in the past: «She comported herself with dignity».
fact: Comport is a formal word, so it is used in serious writing and old stories, not usually in everyday chat.
exercise: «The guests comported themselves politely at the royal dinner.» What does comported themselves mean? | Left the table early | Ate too much cake | *Behaved in a polite, proper way | Argued loudly with everyone
exercise: «Her story comports with the photos we found.» What does comports with mean? | Gets lost in | Argues against | Is written in pencil | *Agrees with, or matches
exercise: Dad says, «Please comport yourself well at the wedding.» What does he want? | Leave early | Dance all night | *Behave properly | Take lots of photos
test: «The class was told to comport themselves with care in the museum.» What does comport mean here? | Eat lunch in the gallery | *Behave in a careful, proper way | Run around loudly | Sleep on the benches
test: «His excuse does not comport with the facts.» What does this tell you? | His excuse is very old | *His excuse does not agree with the facts | His excuse was written down | His excuse is very short and funny
exam: «In old stories, a knight had to comport himself well at court.» What does comport himself mean? | Ride his horse fast | *Behave well and properly | Leave the castle at night | Fight the dragon
